                        <!-- [...] -->
                      </listBibl>
                    </sourceDesc></egXML></item>
              </list></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="encodingDescription" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Encoding description</head>
          <p part="N"><gi scheme="TEI">encodingDesc</gi> is an optional element, which can be used
            to document the methods and editorial principles which governed the transcription or
            encoding of the lexicographic resource in hand and may also include sets of coded
            definitions used elsewhere in the text. </p>
          <p part="N">For an explanation of how to encode a taxonomy of domain labels to be used for
            encoding usage labels, see section on <ref target="#HierarchicalUsageLabels"
              >hierarchical usage labels</ref>. </p>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="profile-description" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Profile description</head>
          <p part="N">In TEI P5, <gi scheme="TEI">profileDesc</gi> is an optional element, whereas
            in TEI Lex-0, it is required: <gi scheme="TEI">profileDesc</gi> itself requires <gi
              scheme="TEI">langUsage</gi> and <gi scheme="TEI">langUsage</gi> requires at least one
              <gi scheme="TEI">language</gi> element. This is because for any lexical or
            lexicographic resource, it is essential to identify and record the language(s) used as
            part of the resource metadata. </p>
          <p part="N">Regarding the use of the required attribute <att scheme="TEI">role</att> and
            its possible values (<val>objectLanguage</val>, <val>workingLanguage</val>,
              <val>sourceLanguage</val> or <val>targetLanguage</val>), see the specification details
            for <gi scheme="TEI">language</gi>. </p>
          <div xml:id="language-profile" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Language profiles</head>
            <p part="N">Lexicographic resources often deal not only with well-established languages,
              but also with language varieties: historical stages, regional dialects, sociolects,
              writing conventions, or project-specific subsets of a language. TEI Lex-0 requires
              that each language (or variety) used in the resource be identified with a standard
              language tag, and described in a structured way in the TEI header.</p>
            <p part="N">At the level of identification, TEI Lex-0 follows established practice: use
                <ref target="http://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/bcp/bcp47.txt">IETF BCP 47</ref> language
              tags (usually grounded in ISO 639) wherever possible. Because language varieties are
              not exhaustively standardized, projects may need to create <ref
                target="entries.html#naming-your-languages">private-use tags</ref>. If you do so,
              make sure to document them explicitly in the header so that the meaning of each tag is
              clear to both humans and software.</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, language profiles are recorded in <code rend="language-xpath"
                >profileDesc/langUsage</code>. Each <gi scheme="TEI">language</gi> element
              provides:</p>
            <list>
              <item>a required identifier (<att scheme="TEI">ident</att>) and a required functional
                role (<att scheme="TEI">role</att>) such as <val>objectLanguage</val> or
                  <val>workingLanguage</val>;</item>
              <item>one or more human-readable names (<gi scheme="TEI">name</gi>), optionally
                multilingual using <att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att>;</item>
              <item>optional additional identifiers (<gi scheme="TEI">ident</gi>) and descriptive
                metadata that characterize a language variety.</item>
            </list>
            <p part="N">Prior to v0.9.5, which introduced a richer data model for describing
              languages, TEI Lex-0 recommended entering the human-readable name of a language
              directly as a text node of <gi scheme="TEI">language</gi>, for instance: <egXML
                xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" rend="unwrapped"
                xml:base="fragments/language-name-old.xml" valid="true"><language ident="en"
                  role="objectLanguage">English</language></egXML> This remains technically valid
              for backwards compatibility. Moving forward, however, users are strongly encouraged to
              follow this pattern: <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" rend="unwrapped"
                xml:base="fragments/language-name-new.xml" valid="true"><language ident="en"
                  role="objectLanguage"><name>English</name></language></egXML> so that the language
              name can be processed consistently alongside other descriptive dimensions in a way
              that is compatible with ISO 21636. The most common of these dimensions are:</p>
            <list>
              <item><hi rend="italic">space</hi> (where the variety is used), using <gi scheme="TEI"
                  >settingDesc</gi> with <gi scheme="TEI">place</gi> or <gi scheme="TEI"
                  >listPlace</gi>;</item>
              <item><hi rend="italic">time</hi> (when the variety is used), using <gi scheme="TEI"
                  >date</gi> and its dating attributes;</item>
              <item><hi rend="italic">social group</hi> (who uses the variety), using <gi
                  scheme="TEI">personGrp</gi> (and, when needed, <gi scheme="TEI"
                >langKnowledge</gi>);</item>
              <item><hi rend="italic">medium</hi> (how it is communicated), using <gi scheme="TEI"
                  >channel</gi> (e.g. spoken vs. written).</item>
            </list>
            <p part="N"><gi scheme="TEI">language</gi> is a very flexible element in Lex-0. It
              allows you to record language profiles that range from basic identification of
              dictionary's object and working languages to full-blown profiles describing a language
              variety or its speakers.</p>
            <list type="examples">
              <item><desc>Basic language identification (monolingual) </desc><egXML
                  xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><langUsage
                    xml:base="../examples/headers/LanguageProfileExamples.stripped.xml"><language
                      ident="ru-x-lit19c" role="objectLanguage"><name xml:lang="ru">Русский
                        литературный язык XIX века</name><name xml:lang="en">19th-century literary
                        Russian</name><date notBefore="1800" notAfter="1899"/></language><language
                      ident="ru" role="workingLanguage"><name xml:lang="ru">Современный русский
                        язык</name><name xml:lang="en">Contemporary
                    Russian</name></language></langUsage></egXML></item>
              <item><desc>Basic language identification (bilingual)</desc><egXML
                  xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><langUsage
                    xml:base="../examples/headers/LanguageProfileExamples.stripped.xml"><language
                      role="sourceLanguage" ident="yi"><name xml:lang="en">Yiddish</name><name
                        xml:lang="yi">ייִדיש</name></language><language role="targetLanguage"
                      ident="en"><name xml:lang="en">English</name><name xml:lang="yi"
                      >ענגליש</name></language></langUsage></egXML></item>
              <item><desc>Providing multiple standard identifiers (e.g. ISO 639
                  variants)</desc><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"
                    ><langUsage xml:base="../examples/headers/LanguageProfileExamples.stripped.xml"
                      ><language status="active" role="objectLanguage" ident="fr"><ident
                        type="languageIdentifier" subtype="ISO639-3B">fre</ident><ident
                        type="languageIdentifier" subtype="ISO639-3T">fra</ident><ident
                        type="languageIdentifier" subtype="ISO639-2">fr</ident><name
                        type="languageName" role="languageReferenceName" xml:lang="en"
                        >French</name><name type="languageName" xml:lang="fr"
                      >Français</name></language></langUsage></egXML></item>
              <item><desc>Characterizing a variety through the profile of its speaker
                  community</desc><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"
                    ><langUsage xml:base="../examples/headers/LanguageProfileExamples.stripped.xml"
                      ><language role="objectLanguage" ident="ar-x-shawi"><name
                        type="languageVariety">Shawi Arabic </name><personGrp><age>Different
                          ages</age><gender>All genders</gender><faith>Muslim
                          (Sunni)</faith><education>No specific level of education. Any formal
                          education received in Turkish, no formal education in
                          Arabic</education><nationality type="citizenship"
                          >Turkish</nationality><nationality type="ethnicity"
                          >Arab</nationality><residence/><occupation/><socecStatus>The speakers of
                          this variety were formerly sheep and goat rearing
                            nomads.</socecStatus><langKnowledge><langKnown tag="ar-x-shaw"
                            /><langKnown tag="tr"/></langKnowledge><note/></personGrp><channel
                        mode="s">spoken</channel></language></langUsage></egXML></item>
            </list>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="header.revision-description" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Revision description</head>
          <p part="N"><gi scheme="TEI">revisionDesc</gi> is optional in both TEI and TEI Lex-0. The
            element is used to document the revision history of the given file. For each recorded
            revision, one should use the <gi scheme="TEI">change</gi> element , together with the
            appropriate attributes: <att scheme="TEI">when</att> to indicate the date of the
            implemented change, <att scheme="TEI">resp</att> to assign responsibility and <att
              scheme="TEI">n</att> to assign a number to the particular change, </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="entries" xml:base="includes/10__entries.xml" xml:lang="" part="N" org="uniform"
        sample="complete">
        <head>Entries</head>
        <p part="N">An <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> is a basic reference unit in a dictionary: it
          groups together all the information related to a particular lemma. For instance:</p>
        <list type="examples">
          <item><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#OALD1974" valid="true"
                ><entry xml:id="OALD.competitor" type="mainEntry" xml:lang="en"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>competitor</orth><hyph>com|peti|tor</hyph><pron>k@m"petit@(r)</pron></form><gramGrp><gram
                    type="pos">n</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="OALD.competitor.1"><def>person who
                    competes.</def></sense></entry></egXML></item>
          <item><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Московљевић1990"
              valid="true"><entry xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна" xml:lang="sr"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                  ><orth>кру̏на</orth></form><etym>(<cit type="etymon" xml:lang="de"><lang norm="de"
                      xml:lang="sr">нем.</lang><form><orth>Krone</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit
                    type="etymon" xml:lang="la"><lbl xml:lang="sr">из</lbl><lang expand="латински"
                      norm="la">лат.</lang></cit>)</etym><sense xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.1"
                    ><num>1.</num><sense xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.1a"><num>а)</num><def>украс на глави
                      као знак владарске власти;</def></sense><sense xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.1b"
                      ><num>б)</num><usg type="meaningType" expand="фигуративно" norm="figurative"
                      >фиг.</usg><def>владар.</def></sense></sense><sense xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.2"
                    ><num>2.</num><def>новчана јединица у неким европским земљама, разне
                    вредности.</def></sense><sense xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.3"><num>3.</num><def>део
                    лиснатог дрвета изнад стабле (гране и лшће);</def><xr type="synonymy"
                      ><lbl>син.</lbl><ref type="sense">крошња</ref><pc>.</pc></xr></sense><sense
                  xml:id="MM.RSSKJ.круна.4"><num>4.</num><usg type="meaningType"
                    expand="фигуративно" norm="figurative">фиг.</usg><def>врхунац, највиши домет
                    неког рада, забаве.</def></sense></entry></egXML></item>
        </list>
        <div xml:id="entries.mandatory-attributes" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Mandatory attributes</head>
          <p part="N">The TEI Lex-0 schema prescribes two mandatory attributes on <gi scheme="TEI"
              >entry</gi>:</p>
          <list>
            <item><att scheme="TEI">xml:id</att><hi rend="italic">uniquely</hi> identifies the
              element it is associated with;</item>
            <item><att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> identifies the object language of the element it
              is associated with.</item>
          </list>
          <p part="N">In XML, <att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> is inherited from the immediately
            enclosing element or from its closest ancestor that has this attribute. This means that
            in XML not every element needs to have the <att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> attribute. </p>
          <p part="N">TEI Lex-0 recommends that <att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> be attached to
            so-called container elements (such as <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> and <gi scheme="TEI"
              >cit</gi>) rather than individual <gi scheme="TEI">form</gi> elements. </p>
          <p part="N">In addition, TEI Lex-0 privileges <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> as the
            dictionary’s central textual component by requiring both a unique identifier (<att
              scheme="TEI">xml:id</att>) as well as <att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att>. </p>
          <floatingText type="inFocusPanel" xml:id="naming-your-languages">
            <body>
              <head>Naming your languages </head>
              <p part="N"><att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> identifies the object language of the
                element it is associated with. The language ‘tag’ (i.e. the value of this attribute)
                must follow <ref target="http://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/bcp/bcp47.txt">IETF BCP
                  47</ref>, the Internet Engineering Task Force's best-practice document outlining
                standard identifiers for labeling language content. To learn more about what
                language tag is appropriate for your project, check out W3C's useful resource on
                  <ref
                  target="https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-choosing-language-tags.en"
                  >choosing language tags</ref>. </p>
              <p part="N">If the language or language variety you are working on is not covered by
                BCP 47, make sure to follow the syntax of Private Use Tags described in BCP 47 <ref
                  target="https://tools.ietf.org/html/bcp47#section-2.2.7">Section 2.2.7</ref> when
                creating one. Do this only if you are absolutely certain that no standard tag exists
                for your object language. </p>
              <p part="N">If you have created a "private" language tag, you can validate it (in
                terms of its structural well-formedness and validity) using the <ref
                  target="https://schneegans.de/lv/"> BCP 47 validator</ref>.</p>
              <p part="N">Language tags containing private-use subtags should be documented in the
                TEI header, specifically using one or more <gi scheme="TEI">language</gi> elements
                grouped under <gi scheme="TEI">langUsage</gi> inside <gi scheme="TEI"
                  >profileDesc</gi>:</p>
              <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><profileDesc>
                  <langUsage>
                    <language ident="mix" role="objectLanguage">
                      <name xml:lang="en">Mixtepec Mixtec</name>
                    </language>
                    <language ident="mix-x-YCNY" role="objectLanguage">
                      <name xml:lang="en">Yucanany Mixtec</name>
                    </language>
                  </langUsage>
                </profileDesc></egXML>
            </body>
          </floatingText>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="entries.grammatical-properties" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Grammatical properties</head>
          <p part="N">Grammatical properties of lexical entries should be specified in <code
              rend="language-xpath">entry/gramGrp/gram</code>. This <gi scheme="TEI">gram</gi>
            element will typically specify the part-of-speech of the entry:</p>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:lang="en"
              type="mainEntry" xml:id="on"><form type="lemma"><orth>on</orth></form><gramGrp><gram
                  type="pos">prep</gram></gramGrp><!--...--></entry></egXML>
          <p part="N">Notes:</p>
          <list type="ordered">
            <item>Grammatical properties of the entry as a whole should <hi rend="italic">not</hi>
              be specified in <code rend="language-xpath">entry/form[@type="lemma"]/gramGrp</code>. </item>
            <item><code rend="language-xpath">entry/form/gramGrp</code> should be used only if a
              particular form (a dialectal variant, for instance) has different grammatical
              properties from the lemma; or to indicate the grammatical properties of the inflected
              form which clearly deviate from the lemma. </item>
            <item>For entries which group grammatical homonyms inside single entries (e.g. in
              English dictionaries which do not have separate entries for conversion pairs of nouns
              and verbs, such as <hi rend="bold">run</hi> or <hi rend="bold">aid</hi> see the
              discussion under <ref target="#nested-entries-vs-multiple-senses">Nested entries vs.
                multiple-senses</ref>. </item>
          </list>
          <div xml:id="entries.grammatical-properties.typology-of-gram" part="N" org="uniform"
            sample="complete">
            <head>Typology of <gi scheme="TEI">gram</gi></head>
            <p part="N">The <hi rend="italic">TEI Guidelines</hi> define: <list>
                <item>seven <hi rend="italic">specific</hi> elements which can be used to mark up
                  particular grammatical properties:<gi scheme="TEI">case</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI"
                    >gen</gi> (for gender), <gi scheme="TEI">iType</gi> (for inflection type), <gi
                    scheme="TEI">mood</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">number</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">per</gi>
                  (for person) and <gi scheme="TEI">tns</gi> (for tense); and </item>
                <item>one <hi rend="italic">general</hi> element (<gi scheme="TEI">gram</gi>) which
                  can be used to encode different kinds of grammatical properties.</item>
              </list></p>
            <p part="N">The <hi rend="italic">Guidelines</hi> themselves do not explain the
              reasoning behind having two different mechanisms for encoding the same kind of
              information. The two mechanisms are treated as fully interchangeable: see, for
              instance, the first two examples in <ref
                target="https://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/DI.html#DITPGR">Section
                9.3.2</ref>.</p>
            <p part="N">While it is perfectly understandable why marking up grammatical information
              using a number of specific, granular elements can be considered desirable, the current
              situation is less than perfect: <list>
                <item>if both <code rend="language-xml">&lt;pos&gt;prep&lt;/pos&gt;</code> and <code
                    rend="language-xml">&lt;gram type="pos"&gt;prep&lt;/gram&gt;</code> are
                  possible, and if both mean exactly the same thing, the choice about how to encode
                  grammatical information will always be partially arbitrary;</item>
                <item>the <hi rend="italic">specific</hi> grammatical elements in TEI cover some
                  important grammatical categories, but are certainly not exhaustive: for instance,
                  Slavic dictionaries will, as a rule, indicate aspect (imperfective or perfective)
                  as the defining grammatical property of verbs, yet there is no specific element
                  for: <gi scheme="TEI">aspect</gi> in TEI.</item>
                <item>if there are no <hi rend="italic">specific</hi> elements for every possible
                  grammatical category, mixing specific and general elements (for instance <code
                    rend="language-xml">&lt;pos&gt;v.&lt;/pos&gt;</code> and <code
                    rend="language-xml">&lt;gram type="aspect"&gt;imperf.&lt;/gram&gt;</code> within
                  the same entry and/or dictionary will most likely further complicate data
                  processing and data interoperability.</item>
              </list></p>
            <p part="N">Considering the goals of TEI Lex-0 to serve as a common baseline and target
              format for transforming and comparing different lexical resources, we have decided to
              do away with the specific elements for grammatical properties. Instead, we recommend
              the use of typed <gi scheme="TEI">gram</gi> elements. This is a decision that wasn't
              taken lightly and one which solicited a great deal of discussion. It goes without
              saying that TEI itself will continue to support both mechanisms and that an XSLT
              transformation from <code rend="language-xml">&lt;pos&gt;prep&lt;/pos&gt;</code> to
                <code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram type="pos"&gt;prep&lt;/gram&gt;</code> for those
              who want to convert their dictionaries to TEI Lex-0 would be easily accomplished. </p>
            <p part="N">The following table shows a mapping between the specific TEI elements and
              the typed <gi scheme="TEI">gram</gi> elements in TEI Lex-0:<table rows="6" cols="2"
                rend="pure-table pure-table-bordered">
                <head>Mapping between specific elements in TEI and the generalized mechanism in TEI
                  Lex-0</head>
                <row role="label" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">TEI</cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">TEI Lex-0</cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;pos&gt;n.&lt;/pos&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code>&lt;gram
                      type="pos"&gt;n.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;case&gt;acc.&lt;/case&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="case"&gt;acc.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;gen&gt;f.&lt;/gen&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="gender"&gt;f.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;iType&gt;7&lt;/iType&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="inflectionType"&gt;7&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;mood&gt;indic.&lt;/mood&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="mood"&gt;indic.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;number&gt;sg.&lt;/number&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="number"&gt;sg.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;per&gt;3rd&lt;/per&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="person"&gt;3rd&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;tns&gt;aorist&lt;/tns&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="tense"&gt;aorist&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml"
                      >&lt;colloc&gt;de&lt;/tns&gt;</code></cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="colloc"&gt;de&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">-</cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="aspect"&gt;imperf.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">-</cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="valency"&gt;intr.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">-</cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="construction"&gt;[+conj.]&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
                <row role="data" rows="1" cols="1">
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1">-</cell>
                  <cell role="data" rows="1" cols="1"><code rend="language-xml">&lt;gram
                      type="degree"&gt;comp.&lt;/gram&gt;</code></cell>
                </row>
              </table></p>
            <p part="N">Note: See also next section on <ref target="#collocates"
              >Collocates</ref>.</p>
            <p part="N">TEI5 is missing a specific element for encoding the grammatical aspect of
              verbs (for values such as <code>perfective</code>, <code>imperfective</code>) and
              valency (for values such as <code>transitive</code>, <code>intransitive</code>,
                <code>reflexive</code>, and <code>impersonal</code>). TEI Lex-0 is therefore
              introducing two suggested grammatical types: <code rend="language-xpath"
                >gram[@type="aspect"]</code> and <code rend="language-xpath"
                >gram[@type="valency"]</code>for encoding such values in dictionaries.</p>
            <p part="N">The attribute values for <code rend="language-xpath">gram[@type]</code> are
              a semi-closed list: this means that we will discuss and adopt additional values as
              demonstrated by examples from dictionaries that are encoded by members of our
              community. </p>
            <p part="N">If your dictionary has grammatical labels that do not fit into the above
              categories, do let us know by <ref
                target="https://github.com/DARIAH-ERIC/lexicalresources/issues/new">filing a ticket
                on GitHub</ref>. </p>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="entries.grammatical-properties.collocates" part="N" org="uniform"
            sample="complete">
            <head>Collocates</head>
            <p part="N">The <hi rend="italic">TEI Guidelines</hi> define a specific element <gi
                scheme="TEI">colloc</gi> (collocate) for marking up "any sequence of words that
              co-occur with the headword with significant frequency." The prototypical example from
              the <hi rend="italic">Guidelines</hi> is this: <egXML
                xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false"
                      ><entry><form><orth>médire</orth></form><gramGrp><colloc>de</colloc></gramGrp></entry></egXML></p>
            <p part="N">In line with the simplification of the elements used to describe grammatical
              properties in dictionaries, TEI Lex-0 recommends the use of <code rend="language-xml"
                >&lt;gram type="collocate"&gt;&lt;/gram&gt;</code> to encode these phenomena, i.e.:
                &gt;<egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry
                  xml:lang="fr" xml:id="DDLF.médire"><form type="lemma"
                      ><orth>médire</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="collocate"
                  >de</gram></gramGrp></entry></egXML></p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, we make a distinction between purely lexical collocates (as in
              médire <hi rend="italic">de</hi>) and various types of grammatical co-occurrences,
              differently referred to in the literature as rection, government, dependency etc. The
              suggested value for this type of grammatical co-occurrence in TEI Lex-0 is <code
                rend="language-xml">&lt;gram type="construction"&gt;&lt;/gram&gt;</code><egXML
                xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><gramGrp><gram
                    type="construction">[+ conj.]</gram></gramGrp></egXML></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="entries.deprecated-elements" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Deprecated entry-like elements</head>
          <p part="N">The current TEI Guidelines define five different container elements that may
            serve as grouping devices for entry-level lexical information:</p>
          <list>
            <item><gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi>: contains a single <hi rend="italic">structured
                entry</hi> in any kind of lexical resource, such as a dictionary or lexicon.</item>
            <item><gi scheme="TEI">entryFree</gi>: contains a single <hi rend="italic">unstructured
                entry</hi> in any kind of lexical resource, such as a dictionary or lexicon.</item>
            <item><gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi>: groups a sequence of entries within any kind of
              lexical resource, such as a dictionary or lexicon which function as a single unit, for
              example a set of homographs.</item>
            <item><gi scheme="TEI">re</gi>: (related entry) contains a dictionary entry for a
              lexical item related to the headword, such as a compound phrase or derived form,
              embedded inside a larger entry.</item>
            <item><gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi>: (homograph) groups information relating to one
              homograph within an entry</item>
          </list>
          <p part="N">These five elements can be used to distinguish different types of entries
            along two conceptual axes:</p>
          <list>
            <item>Structured vs. unstructured entries, i. e. entries that can readily be represented
              (in the lexical view) in the spirit of the TEI Guideline’s Dictionary Chapter (<gi
                scheme="TEI">entry</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">re</gi>) vs. entries that for some reason
              violate the generic content model of <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> or <gi scheme="TEI"
                >re</gi> and thus have to be represented more freely (<gi scheme="TEI"
                >entryFree</gi>). A third category in this respect are entries that exhibit a highly
              reduced amount of lexical content while this content is still of essentially
              entry-like nature (<gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi>). </item>
            <item>Containing vs. contained entries: entries may contain additional lexical
              information that can be conceived as an additional dictionary entry in its own right.
              Specifically, <gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi> may contain <gi scheme="TEI"
              >entry</gi>, and <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> in turn may contain <gi scheme="TEI"
                >re</gi> to represent the embedding of lexical entries on three distinct levels. Due
              to <gi scheme="TEI">re</gi> being allowed to be used recursively, the number of levels
              for representing entry-like lexical information inside other such blocks is
              effectively unrestricted. At the same time, two different mechanism can be used to
              create homographic entries: <gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi> containing multiple <gi
                scheme="TEI">entry</gi> elements; or <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> containing multiple
                <gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi> elements. </item>
          </list>
          <div xml:id="entries.deprecated-elements.hom" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head><gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi></head>
            <p part="N">Making a clear difference between a situation where an entry has to be split
              into two or more homonyms and one where these differences correspond to a semantic
              alternation is lexicographically difficult. Still, the main danger in keeping both
              possibilities in the representation of a lexical entry in a digital lexicon is to
              introduce a systematic structural ambiguity as to where the appropriate information is
              to be found. We thus deprecate <gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi> altogether in the present
              recommendation and have this element replaced by the nested <gi scheme="TEI"
                >entry</gi> construct.</p>
            <p part="N">For instance, the following example from the TEI <hi rend="italic"
                >Guidelines</hi>:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false"
                    ><entry><form><orth>bray</orth><pron>breI</pron></form><hom><gramGrp><gram
                      type="pos">n</gram></gramGrp><sense><def>cry of an ass; sound of a
                      trumpet.</def></sense></hom><hom><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                      >vt</gram><subc>VP2A</subc></gramGrp><sense><def>make a cry or sound of this
                      kind.</def></sense></hom></entry></egXML>
            <p part="N">would in TEI Lex-0 be represented as:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry type="mainEntry"
                xml:id="bray" xml:lang="en" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
                  type="lemma"><orth>bray</orth><pron>brel</pron></form><entry xml:id="bray_n"
                  xml:lang="en" type="homonymicEntry"><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                    >n</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="bray_n.1"><def>cry of an
                    ass</def></sense><pc>;</pc><sense xml:id="bray_n.2"><def>sound of a
                      trumpet</def></sense><pc>.</pc></entry><entry xml:id="bray_vt" xml:lang="en"
                  type="homonymicEntry"><gramGrp><gram type="pos">vt</gram><gram
                      type="inflectionType">VP2A</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="bray_vt.1"><def>make
                      a cry or sound of this kind</def></sense><pc>.</pc></entry></entry></egXML>
            <p part="N">In a similar fashion, consider this entry from the <hi rend="italic"
                >Dictionary of the Portuguese Language</hi> by Morais:</p>
            <list type="examples">
              <item><figure>
                  <graphic width="100%"
                    url="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/45368069/196040540-8e0253c2-a4cc-4a55-8ad0-0dc461945978.png"
                  />
                </figure><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Morais1789"
                  valid="true"><entry xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR" type="mainEntry" xml:lang="pt"
                    xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><entry
                      xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR-vt" type="homonymicEntry" xml:lang="pt"><form
                        type="lemma"><orth>JANTAR</orth></form><metamark function="lemmaDelimiter"
                        >,</metamark><gramGrp><gram type="pos" norm="VERB">v.</gram><gram
                          type="voice">at.</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR.s.1"
                          ><def>comer ao meio dia , ou comer depois de
                      almoçar.</def></sense></entry><entry xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR-n"
                      type="homonymicEntry" xml:lang="pt"><form type="lemma"
                        ><orth>JANTAR</orth></form><metamark function="lemmaDelimiter"
                          >,</metamark><gramGrp><gram type="pos" norm="NOUN">ſ.</gram><gram
                          type="gen">m.</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR.s.2"
                          ><def>a ſegunda das tres comidas regulares do dia, entre o almoço , e
                          aceia , ou antes da merenda.</def></sense><pc>.</pc><metamark
                        function="senseDelimiter">§</metamark><sense
                        xml:id="MORAIS.1.DLP.JANTAR.s.3"><def>Porção de dinheiro , que as Villas , e
                          Cidades davão aos Reis , quando hião de correição para ſuſtento de ſua
                          comitiva</def></sense><pc>.</pc><bibl type="attestation"
                        source="#M._L._Monarchia_Luſitana"><title>M. Luſ.</title><citedRange
                          unit="volume">t. 5</citedRange><citedRange unit="folium">f.
                          53</citedRange><citedRange unit="chapter">cap.
                      27</citedRange></bibl></entry></entry></egXML></item>
            </list>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="entries.deprecated-elements.superEntry" part="N" org="uniform"
            sample="complete">
            <head><gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi></head>
            <p part="N">By making <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> recursive, TEI Lex-0 has eliminated
              the need for grouping entries with <gi scheme="TEI">superEntry</gi>.</p>
            <p part="N">This is especially important for traditional root-based dictionaries, which
              start with the root as the main headword, followed by full-fledged lexicographic
              entries of derived headwords.</p>
            <list type="examples">
              <item><figure>
                  <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/d4QTXla.png"/>
                </figure><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Almonjid2014"
                  valid="true"><entry type="wordFamily" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="syj"
                    xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="root"
                      ><orth>سيج</orth></form><pc>:</pc><!-- To fence (verb) --><entry
                      type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="syj1"><form type="lemma"
                          ><orth>سيّج</orth></form><sense xml:id="syj1_sense1"><cit type="example"
                            ><quote>الكرم</quote></cit><pc>:</pc><def>جعل له
                        سياجا</def></sense><pc>٠</pc></entry><!-- A fence (noun) --><entry
                      type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="syj2"><form type="lemma"
                          ><orth>السياج</orth></form><form type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram
                            type="number" value="plural">ج</gram></gramGrp><form type="variant"
                            ><orth>سيَاجات</orth></form><lbl>و</lbl><form type="variant"
                            ><orth>أسْوِجة</orth></form><lbl>و</lbl><form type="variant"
                            ><orth>أَسْوِجة</orth></form><lbl>و</lbl><form type="variant"
                            ><orth>سُوج</orth></form></form><pc>:</pc><sense xml:id="syj2_sense1"
                          ><def>الحائط</def></sense><pc>||</pc><sense xml:id="syj2_sense2"><def>ما
                          أُحيط بهِ على شيءٍ كالكرم و
                      النخل</def></sense></entry><pc>٠</pc><!-- A kind of fish --><entry
                      type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="syj3"><form type="lemma"
                          ><orth>السيْجان</orth></form><pc>(</pc><usg type="domain" value="animal"
                        >ح</usg><pc>)</pc><pc>:</pc><sense xml:id="syj3_sense1"><def>نوع من
                          السمك</def></sense></entry></entry></egXML></item>
              <item><figure>
                  <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/Lb2sxyo.png"/>
                </figure><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Almonjid2014"
                  valid="true"><entry type="wordFamily" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="shahama"
                    xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="root"
                      ><orth>شهم</orth></form><pc>:</pc><entry type="wordfamily" xml:lang="ar"
                      xml:id="shahama1"><num>١ــ</num><entry type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar"
                        xml:id="shahama1_1"><form type="lemma"><orth>شَهَمَ</orth></form><form
                          type="scheme"><orth>ـَ</orth></form><form type="inflected"><form
                            type="variant"><orth>شَهْمًا</orth></form><lbl>و</lbl><form
                            type="variant"><orth>شُهُمًا</orth></form></form><sense
                          xml:id="shahama1_1_sense1"><cit type="example"
                            ><quote>الفرسَ</quote></cit><pc>:</pc><def>زجره</def></sense><pc>||</pc><lbl>و</lbl><sense
                          xml:id="shahama1_1_sense2"><cit type="example"><quote>ــ
                            الرجُل</quote></cit><pc>:</pc><def>افزعه</def></sense></entry><pc>٠</pc><entry
                        type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="shahama1_2"><form type="lemma"
                            ><orth>اَلمشْهوم</orth></form><pc>٠:</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama1_2_sense1"
                      ><def>المذعور</def></sense></entry></entry><entry type="wordFamily"
                      xml:lang="ar" xml:id="shahama2"><num>٢٠ ــ</num><entry type="mainEntry"
                        xml:lang="ar" xml:id="shahama2_1"><form type="lemma"
                          ><orth>شَهُم</orth></form><form type="scheme"><orth>ـُـ</orth></form><form
                          type="inflected"><form type="variant"
                            ><orth>شَهَامةً</orth></form><lbl>و</lbl><form type="variant"
                              ><orth>شُهُومَةُُ</orth></form></form><lbl>:</lbl><sense
                          xml:id="shahama2_1_sense1"><def> كان
                        شهْمًا</def></sense></entry><pc>٠</pc><entry type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar"
                        xml:id="shahama2_2"><form type="lemma"><orth>الشَهْم</orth></form><form
                          type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram type="number" value="plural"
                            >ج</gram></gramGrp><orth>شِهام</orth></form><pc>:</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama2_2_sense1"><def>الذكيّ
                          الفؤاد</def></sense><pc>||</pc><sense xml:id="shahama2_2_sense2"
                            ><def>السيِّد النافذ الحكم</def></sense><pc>||</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama2_2_sense3"><lbl>وــ</lbl><form type="inflected"
                                ><gramGrp><gram type="number" value="plural"
                              >ج</gram></gramGrp><orth>شُهُم</orth></form><pc>:</pc><def>الفرس
                            النشيط السريع القويّ</def></sense></entry><pc>٠</pc><entry
                        type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar" xml:id="shahama2_3"><form type="lemma"
                            ><orth>اَلمَشْهُوم</orth></form><pc>*:</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama2_3_sense1"><def>الذكيّ
                      الفؤاد</def></sense></entry></entry><entry type="wordFamily" xml:lang="ar"
                      xml:id="shahama3"><num>٠٣ ــ</num><entry type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar"
                        xml:id="shahama3_1"><form type="lemma"><orth>الشَيْهَم</orth></form><form
                          type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram type="number" value="plural"
                            >ج</gram></gramGrp><orth>شَيَهِم</orth></form><pc>(</pc><usg
                          type="domain" value="animal">ح</usg><pc>)</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama3_1_sense1"><def>ذَكَر
                        القنافذ</def></sense></entry><pc>٠</pc><entry type="mainEntry" xml:lang="ar"
                        xml:id="shahama3_2"><form type="lemma"
                          ><orth>الشَيْهَمَة</orth></form><pc>:</pc><sense
                          xml:id="shahama3_2_sense1"
                    ><def>العجوز</def></sense></entry></entry></entry></egXML></item>
            </list>
            <p part="N">See also Section on <ref target="#grammatical-properties-in-senses"
                >grammatical properties in senses</ref>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="forms" xml:base="includes/15__forms.xml" xml:lang="" part="N" org="uniform"
        sample="complete">
        <head>Forms</head>
        <p part="N">The current TEI <hi rend="italic">Guidelines</hi> allows for an extremely wide
          range of encoding possibilities for written and spoken forms. In the discussion which
          follows, we suggest ways in which the elements, in particular <gi scheme="TEI">form</gi>,
          can be constrained. We give examples of use types not covered by the <hi rend="italic"
            >Guidelines</hi>, and propose some extensions.</p>
        <div xml:id="forms.inheritance" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>A note on inheritance</head>
          <p part="N">We assume that in order to determine the complete properties of an element
            inside the entry tree, the principle of default inheritance applies, e.g. grammatical
            properties of a form are determined by collecting the sibling <gi scheme="TEI"
              >gramGrp</gi> of the ancestor-or-self of the focus element, where the superordinate
            grammatical properties can be overwritten by the lower-level properties. This principle
            is relatively straightforward in the case of grammatical properties, but more complex
            for the word paradigm, esp. in cases of variant forms. For more information c.f. Ide et
            al. (<ref
              target="https://www.kilgarriff.co.uk/Publications/2000-IdeKilgRomary-Euralex.pdf"
                ><hi>2000</hi></ref>) and Erjavec et al. (<ref
              target="http://www.lrec-conf.org/proceedings/lrec2000/html/summary/335.htm"
                ><hi>2000</hi></ref>).</p>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.lemmas" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Lemmas</head>
          <p part="N">The form element should always be qualified by its <att scheme="TEI"
              >type</att>. The lemma (i.e. headword) form should be encoded as <code
              rend="language-xpath">form[@type="lemma"]</code>.</p>
          <p part="N">If it is necessary to specify the grammatical properties of the lemma form
            itself (as opposed to the grammatical properties of the entry), this is described by
              <code rend="language-xpath">entry/form[@type="lemma"]/gramGrp</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.inflected" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Inflected forms</head>
          <p part="N">Dictionaries often include additional forms next to the lemma. In English,
            these are used to specify irregular forms, such as “corpus / corpora” or “take / took”,
            whereas in inflectionally rich languages they are often used to help the user determine
            the correct paradigm of the word. </p>
          <p part="N">Such inflected forms should be encoded in <code rend="language-xpath"
              >entry/form[@type="inflected"]</code>, e.g.:</p>
          <figure>
            <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/JxXgsjl.jpg"/>
          </figure>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Chambers2014" valid="true"><entry
              xml:lang="en" xml:id="CH.go1" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
                type="lemma"><orth>go</orth><pron>gō</pron></form><lbl rend="sup"
                  >1</lbl><gramGrp><gram type="pos">vi</gram></gramGrp><pc>(</pc><form
                type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram type="participle"
                  >prp</gram></gramGrp><orth>gō'ing</orth></form><pc>;</pc><form type="inflected"
                    ><gramGrp><gram type="participle"
                  >pap</gram></gramGrp><orth>gone</orth><pron>gon</pron><note>(see separate
                  entries)</note></form><pc>;</pc><form type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram
                    type="participle">pat</gram></gramGrp><orth>went</orth><note>(supplied from <xr
                    type="related"><ref type="entry">wend</ref></xr>)</note></form><pc>;</pc><form
                type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram type="person">3rd</gram><gram type="tense"
                    >pers</gram><gram type="number">sing</gram><gram type="tense">pres</gram><gram
                    type="mood"
              >indicative</gram></gramGrp><orth>goes</orth></form><pc>;</pc><!--...--></entry></egXML>
          <p part="N">Or take this example: <hi rend="bold">abeceda, -y</hi>: in Czech, "-y" is a
            genitive singular suffix for feminine nouns. We can mark-up the grammatical properties
            of the suffix, while providing the full form of the noun as well:</p>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry type="mainEntry"
              xml:lang="cz" xml:id="en000008" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
                type="lemma" xml:id="en000008.hw1"><orth>abeceda</orth></form><pc>,</pc><form
                type="inflected"><gramGrp><gram type="case" value="genitiv"/><gram type="number"
                    value="singular"/><gram type="gender" value="feminine"/></gramGrp><orth
                  extent="suffix" expand="abecedy">-y</orth></form><!--...--></entry></egXML>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.paradigms" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Paradigms</head>
          <p part="N">When several inflected forms can be present next to the lemma, these can be
            embedded into <code rend="langauge-xpath">entry/form[@type="paradigm"]</code>. The
            decision on whether to use this extra element depends on the particular dictionary and
            language.</p>
          <p part="N">The other use case for paradigms is when the full inflectional paradigm of the
            word is embedded in the entry, i.e. when the dictionary also includes all the word-forms
            of the words covered, which can be useful for example in machine processing. </p>
          <p part="N">An entry may contain several paradigms, e.g. a partial one for humans and a
            full one for machines, or one for each stem of a verb. Each paradigm type should be
            distinguished by the <att scheme="TEI">subtype</att> attribute.</p>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="perder"
              xml:lang="es" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                  ><orth>perder</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos">verb</gram></gramGrp><form
                type="paradigm" subtype="present"><form type="inflected"
                      ><orth>pierdo</orth><gramGrp><gram type="person">1</gram><gram type="number"
                      >sg</gram><gram type="mood">indic</gram><gram type="voice"
                    >active</gram></gramGrp></form><!-- other inflected forms (of present indicative) here --><gramGrp><gram
                    type="tns">present</gram></gramGrp></form><form type="paradigm"
                subtype="preteritum"><form type="inflected"><orth>perdí</orth><gramGrp><gram
                      type="person">1</gram><gram type="number">sg</gram><gram type="mood"
                      >indic</gram><gram type="voice">active</gram></gramGrp></form><gramGrp><gram
                    type="tense">preteritum</gram></gramGrp></form><!--... --></entry></egXML>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.variants" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Variants</head>
          <p part="N">The representation of variation within a form is highly dependant upon the
            specifics of the features of the variation and the way in which they vary. However, as a
            general principle, variation may be encoded as <code rend="language-xpath"
              >form[@type="variant"]</code> and embedded within the parent element for which a
            subordinate feature exhibits variation.</p>
          <div xml:id="forms.variants.orthographic" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Orthographic variation</head>
            <p part="N">Several kinds of orthographic variation may be distinguished. Below, we
              present some of the options with the corresponding examples.</p>
            <p part="N">Spelling variation due to change in language’s orthography convention:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry
                xml:id="Flussschifffahrt" xml:lang="de" type="compound"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"><orth
                    type="segmented"><seg>Fluss</seg><seg>schifffahrt</seg></orth><form
                    type="variant"
                    ><orth><seg>Fluss</seg><pc>-</pc><seg>Schifffahrt</seg></orth></form><form
                    type="variant"><orth notAfter="1996"
                      ><seg>Fluß</seg><seg>schiffahrt</seg></orth><usg type="temporal">Vor 1996
                      Rechtschreibung Reform</usg></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                    >noun</gram></gramGrp></form><!--...--></entry></egXML>
            <p part="N">The following example is from American English in which due to the lack of
              official conventions for transliteration of Arabic orthography to the English (Latin)
              script, the initial vowel in the surname ‘Osama Bin Laden’ varies between ‘O’ and
              ‘U’:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="Osama"
                xml:lang="en" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"><pron
                    notation="ipa"><seg xml:id="ousma" corresp="#usma #osma"
                      >ow."sa.ma</seg><seg>bɪn</seg><seg>ˈlaːdn̹</seg></pron><form type="variant"
                      ><orth type="transliterated"><seg xml:id="osma" corresp="#usma #ousma"
                        >Osama</seg><seg>Bin</seg><seg>Laden</seg></orth></form><form type="variant"
                      ><orth type="transliterated"><seg xml:id="usma" corresp="#osma #ousma"
                        >Usama</seg><seg>Bin</seg><seg>Laden</seg></orth></form></form><!--...--></entry></egXML>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="forms.variants.phonetic" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Phonetic variation</head>
            <p part="N">In this example, the entry contains the single orthographic form as a direct
              child of the lemma and phonetic transcriptions of the two roughly equally used variant
              pronunciations of the word 'caramel' from American English.</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xml:id="caramel-prons" valid="true"
                ><entry xml:id="caramel-en" xml:lang="en-US"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>caramel</orth><form type="variant"><pron notation="ipa"
                    >'keɹə"mɛl</pron></form><form type="variant"><pron notation="ipa"
                    >'kaɹmɫ̩</pron></form></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                >noun</gram></gramGrp><!-- ... --></entry></egXML>
            <floatingText type="inFocusPanel" xml:id="multiple-variants">
              <body>
                <head>multiple variants</head>
                <p part="N">In the example <ref target="#caramel-prons">above</ref>, one could have
                  chosen to mark up two different pronunciations using two <gi scheme="TEI"
                    >pron</gi> elements inside the <code rend="language-xpath"
                    >form[@type="lemma"]</code>. Considering, however, that each individual
                  pronunciation could, in theory, be further qualified, for instance, by a <gi
                    scheme="TEI">usg</gi> note, indicating the geographic area in which the said
                  pronunciation is used, TEI Lex-0 recommends that multiple variants, whether
                  orthographic or orthoepic, be contained each in its own <gi scheme="TEI">form</gi>
                  element.</p>
              </body>
            </floatingText>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="forms.variants.dialectal" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Regional or dialectal variation</head>
            <p part="N"> In the following example from Mixtepec-Mixtec, there is variation in the
              form of the word for the city of Oaxaca between speakers from the village of Yucanany
              and the rest of the speakers. Since the Yucanany variety makes up only a small portion
              of the speakers of the language, this case of variation is represented as an embedded
                <code rend="language-xpath">form[@type="variant"]</code> within the lemma. Note the
              use of <code rend="language-xpath">usg[@type="geographic"]/placeName</code> to
              explicitly specify this feature in addition to the use of the private language subtag
                (<code rend="language-xpath">@xml:lang="mix-x-YCNY"</code>) as per <ref>BCP
              47</ref>.</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="Oaxaca-MIX"
                xml:lang="mix" type="compound" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
                  type="lemma"><orth><seg>Ñuu</seg><seg>Ntua</seg></orth><pron notation="ipa"
                      ><seg>ɲùù</seg><seg>nd̪ùá</seg></pron><form type="variant"
                    xml:lang="mix-x-YCNY"><orth>Ntua</orth><pron notation="ipa">nd̪ùá</pron><usg
                      type="geographic"> Yucanany </usg></form></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                    >locationNoun</gram></gramGrp><!--...--></entry></egXML>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.condensed" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Condensed forms</head>
          <p part="N">In some lexicographic traditions, it is common to encounter dictionary entries
            that conflate two or more distinct lemmas into a single entry headword. A frequent
            example of this is the combination of a base verb and its reflexive variant in Savic
            languages. While such pairings may suggest morphological or derivational relationships,
            they are more than just inflectional variants: they can sometimes represent distinct
            lexical items, each with its own semantics and usage. </p>
          <p part="N">To illustrate this phenomenon, consider the Old Czech verb <code>leleti</code>
            and its reflexive counterpart <code>leleti sě</code>. These can be presented in a print
            dictionary in a condensed, shorthand form such as <code>leleti (sě)</code>.</p>
          <figure>
            <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/Dm57uLO.png" rend="padded"/>
          </figure>
          <p part="N">In English, this would be comparable to a hypothetical lemma <code>ask
              (oneself)</code> combining <code>ask</code> (“to pose a question”) and <code>ask
              oneself</code> (“to wonder, to reflect”) in a single entry as one condensed lemma.</p>
          <p part="N">Because bracketed constructions such as <code>leleti (sě)</code> do not
            represent naturally occurring word forms, we recommend treating these condensed forms as
            labels (<gi scheme="TEI">lbl</gi>) rather than lemmas. From an encoding perspective,
            this means using <code rend="language-xpath">lbl[@type="condensedLemma"]</code> to
            represent the compacted headword for display or identification purposes, while defining
            each of the component lemmas separately using individual <gi scheme="TEI">form</gi>
            elements:</p>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#MSS" valid="true"><entry
              xml:lang="cs-x-old" type="mainEntry" xml:id="MSS.leleti"
              xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><lbl type="condensedLemmas">leleti
                (sě)</lbl><form type="lemma" resp="#boris"><orth value="leleti"/></form><form
                type="lemma" resp="#boris"><orth value="leleti sě"/></form><metamark
                function="delimiter">, </metamark><lbl type="condensedInflectedForms">-eju, -éš
                (sě)</lbl><form type="inflected" resp="#boris"><orth value="leleju"/><gramGrp><gram
                    type="person" value="1"/><gram type="number" value="singular"/><gram
                    type="tense" value="present"/><gram type="mood" value="indicative"
                /></gramGrp></form><form type="inflected" resp="#boris"><orth value="lelejéš"
                    /><gramGrp><gram type="person" value="2"/><gram type="number" value="singular"
                    /><gram type="tense" value="present"/><gram type="mood" value="indicative"
                  /></gramGrp></form><form type="inflected"><orth value="leleju sě" resp="#boris"
                    /><gramGrp><gram type="person" value="1"/><gram type="number" value="singular"
                    /><gram type="tense" value="present"/><gram type="mood" value="indicative"
                    /><gram type="valency" value="reflexive"/></gramGrp></form><form
                type="inflected"><orth value="lelejéš sě" resp="#boris"/><gramGrp><gram
                    type="person" value="2"/><gram type="number" value="singular"/><gram
                    type="tense" value="present"/><gram type="mood" value="indicative"/><gram
                    type="valency" value="reflexive"/></gramGrp></form><gramGrp><gram type="aspect"
                  expand="nedokonavé" value="imperfective">ned.</gram><gram type="pos" value="verb"
                /></gramGrp><sense xml:id="MSS.leleti.sense.1"><def>vlnit (se), kolébat
                (se)</def></sense></entry></egXML>
          <p part="N">Above, the <att scheme="TEI">value</att> attribute is used to supply
            reconstructed or editorially inferred lemma forms that do <hi rend="italic">not</hi>
            appear explicitly in the print source. This distinction is crucial for digital
            representations of legacy lexica, where not all valid headwords are explicitly listed in
            the original. In TEI Lex-0, it is common to encode lemmas that are not physically
            present in the printed source using the <att scheme="TEI">value</att> attribute on <gi
              scheme="TEI">orth</gi>, rather than placing the text directly as content within the
            element.</p>
          <floatingText type="inFocusPanel" xml:id="indexing-condensed-lemmas">
            <body>
              <head>indexing condensed lemmas</head>
              <p part="N">If we encode regular lemmas (those present in the source) as <code
                  rend="language-xpath">//form[@type='lemma']/orth</code> and reconstructed lemmas
                (those inferred or editorially supplied) as <code rend="language-xpath"
                  >//form[@type='lemma']/orth/@value</code>, we will no longer have a uniform path
                for indexing the dictionary. This is because two kinds of lemmas will reside on
                different XPaths. This, however, is a necessary and principled compromise.</p>
              <p part="N">The suggested encoding and expansion of condensed lemmas allow users and
                systems to reconstruct the full lemma inventory of a dictionary, including
                analytically significant forms absent from the source. At the same time, they make
                explicit which forms are present in the original and which have been editorially
                supplied, thus preserving presentational fidelity to the print source while
                enhancing searchability, machine processing, and downstream applications.</p>
              <p part="N">To support robust querying, it is, therefore, recommended that
                documentation or downstream systems account for this distinction, for instance by
                using the combined XPath expression such as <code rend="language-xpath"
                  >//form[@type='lemma']/orth/text() | //form[@type='lemma']/orth/@value</code> to
                get to the consolidated list of both explicit and implicit lemmas.</p>
            </body>
          </floatingText>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="forms.multiword" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Multiword expressions</head>
          <p part="N">The Dictionary Chapter of the TEI Guidelines is very sparse when it comes to
            recommendations for encoding polylexical units. The only mention of the adjective
            “multi-word” appears in the definition of the element <gi scheme="TEI">term</gi>:
            “contains a single-word, multi-word, or symbolic designation which is regarded as a
            technical term” but this is not relevant for the encoding of polylexical units in
            general-purpose dictionaries.</p>
          <p part="N">TEI includes an element <gi scheme="TEI">colloc</gi> (collocate), which is
            defined as containing “any sequence of words that co-occur with the headword with
            significant frequency” but, in a different example, “colloc” is used as an attribute
            value for the element <gi scheme="TEI">usg</gi> (usage). It is precisely this type of
            ambiguity that TEI Lex-0 is trying to resolve.</p>
          <p part="N">The TEI Guidelines recommend the use of <gi scheme="TEI">re</gi> (related
            entry) to encode “related entries for direct derivatives or inflected forms of the entry
            word, or for compound words, phrases, collocations, and idioms containing the entry
            word” with barely any useful examples, or discussion of how to encode different types of
            polylexical units. TEI Lex-0, on the other hand, does not include <gi scheme="TEI"
              >re</gi>. In TEI Lex-0, <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> was made recursive in order to
            account for nestable entry-like structures without the need to resort to <gi
              scheme="TEI">re</gi>, a differently named element whose content model would be
            indistinguishable from <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi> itself. Eventually, the new content
            model of <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi>, which allows nesting, was adopted by TEI itself
              (<ref target="#Tasovac2020">Tasovac 2020</ref>).</p>
          <p part="N">TODO: explain different types of mwe's from a dict. model perspective
            referring to <ref target="#Tasovac2020">Tasovac 2020</ref>)</p>
          <div xml:id="forms.multiword.collocations" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Collocations</head>
            <p part="N">TODO: explain "lexicographically transparent"</p>
            <figure>
              <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/gcsqdhb.jpg" rend="padded"/>
            </figure>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#DLPC2014" valid="true"><entry
                xml:id="DLPC.descalçar" xml:lang="pt" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"
                  ><!--etc.--><sense xml:id="DLPC.descalçar.1"><!--etc.--><form type="collocations"
                      ><form type="collocation"><orth><ref type="form" scope="currentEntry"
                          value="descalçar"><metamark>+</metamark></ref><seg>as
                          botas</seg></orth><gramGrp><gram type="mwe"
                          value="co-ocorrente_privilegiado"/></gramGrp></form><pc>,</pc><form
                      type="collocation"><orth><ref type="form" scope="currentEntry"
                          value="descalçar"/><seg>as luvas</seg></orth><gramGrp><gram type="mwe"
                          value="co-ocorrente_privilegiado"/></gramGrp></form><pc>,</pc><form
                      type="collocation"><orth><ref type="form" scope="currentEntry"
                          value="descalçar"/><seg>as meias</seg></orth><gramGrp><gram type="mwe"
                          value="co-ocorrente_privilegiado"/></gramGrp></form></form><pc>;</pc><form
                    type="collocations"><form type="collocation"><orth><ref type="form"
                          scope="currentEntry" value="descalçar"><metamark>+</metamark></ref><seg>os
                          sapatos</seg></orth><gramGrp><gram type="mwe"
                          value="co-ocorrente_privilegiado"
                  /></gramGrp></form></form><pc>.</pc></sense></entry></egXML>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="forms.multiword.idiomatic" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Idiomatic expressions</head>
            <p part="N">TODO text ("lexicographically non-transparent")</p>
            <figure>
              <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/VXwxggA.jpg" rend="padded"/>
            </figure>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#DLPC2014" valid="true"><entry
                xml:lang="pt" xml:id="DLPC.bombeiro" type="mainEntry"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>bombeiro</orth></form><!--etc. --><sense xml:id="bombeiro.1"
                    ><!--etc. --><entry xml:id="DLPC.bombeiro_voluntario" xml:lang="pt"
                    type="relatedEntry"><form type="lemma"><orth>bombeiro
                        voluntário</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="mwe" value="combinatória_fixa"
                      /></gramGrp><pc>,</pc><sense xml:id="DLPC.bombeiro_voluntario.1"><def>o que
                        pertence a uma corporação com a obrigatoriedade de acudir a incêndios,
                        acidentes, unicamente por filantropia</def><pc>.</pc></sense></entry><entry
                    xml:id="DLPC.corpo_de_bombeiros" xml:lang="pt" type="relatedEntry"><form
                      type="lemma"><orth><ref type="entry" scope="currentEntry"
                            ><seg>corpo</seg><metamark rend="sup">+</metamark></ref><seg>de
                          bombeiros</seg></orth></form><pc>.</pc></entry></sense><!--etc.--></entry></egXML>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="senses" xml:base="includes/20__senses.xml" xml:lang="" part="N" org="uniform"
        sample="complete">
        <head>Senses</head>
        <p part="N">In the current TEI Dictionary Chapter, the content model of <gi scheme="TEI"
            >entry</gi> allows one to have sense-related information directly within <gi
            scheme="TEI">entry</gi>. TEI Lex-0 proscribes a stricter use of these elements so that
          sense-related information is grouped within the <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> element, in
          accordance with the underlying semasiological model implemented in the TEI <hi
            rend="italic">Guidelines</hi>.</p>
        <p part="N"><gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> should be therefore considered mandatory for any
          dictionary entry that actually provides sense information for the headword. Further in
          this document, we consider some additional specific cases e.g. “referencing” entries
          (entries that simply point to other entries) and inflectional lexica (dictionaries that
          describe word forms only), where <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> is not a mandatory child of
            <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi>.</p>
        <p part="N">As a consequence of making the use of <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> more
          systematic within <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi>, we have seen (see section on <gi
            scheme="TEI">entry</gi>) that some elements are no longer allowed as children of <gi
            scheme="TEI">entry</gi>. We provide here a specific background for each of them:</p>
        <list type="unordered">
          <item><gi scheme="TEI">def</gi> is clearly intended to provide a prose description of a
            meaning within a <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> element and should not appear in any other
            context;</item>
          <item>In the same way, it is recommended that <gi scheme="TEI">cit</gi> be used
            exclusively as a child of <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi>, or when necessary within <gi
              scheme="TEI">dictScrap</gi>;</item>
          <item>The case of <gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi> is peculiar since it provides a subordinate
            organization to an entry which is redundant in relation to what <gi scheme="TEI"
              >sense</gi> allows one to represent. <gi scheme="TEI">hom</gi> is not allowed in TEI
            Lex-0.</item>
        </list>
        <p part="N">Note: In the case one has to deal with information that does not fit a <gi
            scheme="TEI">sense</gi>-based organization, for instance in the process of
          retro-digitizing an existing dictionary source, the use of <gi scheme="TEI">dictScrap</gi>
          is recommended. Further step in the encoding of the lexical content may lead to a more
          precise encoding in a second phase.</p>
        <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> has a mandatory <att scheme="TEI"
            >xml:id</att>.</p>
        <div xml:id="sense.def" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Limiting contexts for <gi scheme="TEI">def</gi></head>
          <p part="N">In the current <hi rend="italic">TEI Guidelines</hi>, <gi scheme="TEI"
              >def</gi> is allowed within the following elements:</p>
          <list type="unordered">
            <item>Module core: <gi scheme="TEI">cit</gi></item>
            <item>Module dictionaries: <gi scheme="TEI">dictScrap</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">entry</gi>,
                <gi scheme="TEI">entryFree</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">etym</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI"
                >hom</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">re</gi>, <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi></item>
            <item>Module namesdates: <gi scheme="TEI">nym</gi></item>
          </list>
          <p part="N">TEI Lex-0 allows the use of <gi scheme="TEI">def</gi> in <gi scheme="TEI"
              >sense</gi> only. All other existing contexts would be implemented by embedding <gi
              scheme="TEI">def</gi> within a <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi>. </p>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="senses.glosses" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Glosses</head>
          <p part="N">In the lexicographic literature, gloss is a rather amorphous category. Zgusta,
            in his classic <hi rend="italic">Manual of Lexicography</hi><ref target="#Zgusta1971"
              >(1971)</ref>, defines it as "any descriptive or explanatory note within the entry"
            which includes "short comments, explanatory remarks, semantic characteristics or
            qualifications" (270). Atkins and Rundell <ref target="#Atkins2008">(2008)</ref> see the
            gloss as "a more informal explanation of the meaning of a multiword expression or
            example (or even part of one) in the entry,[...] chiefly used in monolingual
            dictionaries for learners, to help understanding" (209). While one could argue about the
            statement that this type of lexicographic construct is used "chiefly... in monolingual
            dictionaries for learners", it is certainly the case that glosses are expected to help
            users better understand or more easily locate the particular meaning of a word that they
            are looking up. </p>
          <p part="N">In other words, the prototypical gloss contextualizes and clarifies the
            meaning of the word. Take this example from Zgusta: <list type="ordered">
              <item><hi rend="bold">fugitive</hi> (of persons)</item>
              <item><hi rend="bold">fugitive</hi> (verses)</item>
            </list> Here, glosses are used to signal the meaning of <hi rend="bold">fugitive</hi>:
            in the first sense "fugitive" refers to persons, and in the second example, to verses.
            In TEI Lex-0, this could be represented as: <egXML
              xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="ED.fugitive"
                xml:lang="en"><form type="lemma"><orth>fugitive</orth></form><sense n="1"><gloss>(of
                    persons)</gloss></sense><sense n="2"
              ><gloss>(verses)</gloss></sense></entry></egXML> Glosses, however, are <hi
              rend="italic">not</hi> definitions: one can imagine the above two senses to contain
            proper lexicographic definitions as well: <egXML
              xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="ED.fugitive"
                xml:lang="en"><form type="lemma"><orth>fugitive</orth></form><sense n="1"><gloss>(of
                    persons)</gloss><def>given to, or in the act of, running away from a place,
                    especially to avoid arrest or persecution.</def></sense><sense n="2"
                    ><gloss>(verses)</gloss><def>concerned or dealing with subjects of passing
                    interest; ephemeral, occasional.</def></sense></entry></egXML>Zgusta notes a
            certain amount of overlapping between glosses and other categories, "the most important
            probably being that of the examples" (ibid.) This is especially evident in sense no. 2
            above where "fugitive verses" or "~ verses" could have been used as an example. The
            absence of the lemma or lemma reference in "(verses)" as well as the brackets are a
            clear indicator that the whole construct is not to be read as an example, but rather as
            a semantic signpost for the given sense. </p>
          <p part="N">On sense-distinguishing grammatical properties, see section <ref
              target="#grammatical-properties-in-senses">Grammatical properties in senses</ref></p>
          <div xml:id="senses.glosses.examples" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Glossing examples</head>
            <p part="N">Semantic glosses can occur at different levels of the entry hierarchy. In
              the previous section, we saw examples in which glosses were used as a kind of semantic
              shorthand for an individual sense. They can, however, be used to further qualify
              individual examples in the entry. Take, for instance, this entry from the Longman
              Dictionary of Contemporary English <ref target="#LDOCE2003">(2003)</ref>:</p>
            <p part="N"><hi rend="bold">living</hi> /... / adj <hi rend="bold">1</hi> alive now
              [...] | <hi rend="italic">The sun affects all <hi rend="bold">living</hi> things</hi>
              (=people, animals, and plants). | <hi rend="italic">A <hi rend="bold">living</hi>
                language </hi> (=one that people still use) [….] </p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, this entry would be represented as:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#LDOCE2003" valid="true"><entry
                xml:id="LDOCE.living" xml:lang="en" type="mainEntry"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                  ><orth>living</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos">adj</gram></gramGrp><sense
                  n="1" xml:id="LDOCE.living.1"><num>1</num><def>alive now
                    <!--[...] --></def><metamark>|</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>The sun
                      affects all <ref type="entry" scope="currentEntry">living</ref> things
                        <gloss>(=people, animals, and
                    plants)</gloss>.</quote></cit><metamark>|</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>A
                        <ref type="entry" scope="currentEntry">living</ref> language <gloss>(=one
                        that people still
              use)</gloss><!--[….] --></quote></cit></sense></entry></egXML>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="senses.grammatical-properties" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Grammatical properties</head>
          <p part="N">In some dictionaries, individual dictionary senses may be associated with
            grammatical properties, such as part of speech or gender, that differ from the rest of
            the entry: for instance, a particular sense of a countable noun may be used only in
            plural. In such cases, <gi scheme="TEI">gramGrp</gi> will be naturally placed inside the
            given <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi>: </p>
          <p part="N">Consider, for instance, the second sense of this entry:</p>
          <figure>
            <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/2UJC9Ae.jpg" rend="padded"/>
          </figure>
          <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#DLPC2014"
            xml:id="antepassado-gramGrp-in-sense" valid="true"><sense xml:id="DLPC.antepassado_b_2"
              n="2" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml" xml:lang="pt"><gramGrp><gram
                  type="number">pl.</gram></gramGrp><def>Pessoas anteriormente ao momento
                actual.</def><xr type="synonymy"><ref type="sense">antecessores</ref></xr><xr
                type="antonymy"><ref type="sense">vindouros</ref></xr><cit type="example"
                  ><quote>Hérdamos estes costumes dos nossos antepassados.</quote></cit><cit
                type="example"><quote>Culto dos antepassados.</quote></cit></sense></egXML>
          <div xml:id="senses.grammatical-properties.glosses" part="N" org="uniform"
            sample="complete">
            <head>Grammatical glosses? </head>
            <p part="N">Zgusta also uses "gloss" to describe "grammatical indications in the
              broadest sense of the word" <ref target="#Zgusta1971"> (1971, 240)</ref>, using an
              example familiar from Latin (and many other) dictionaries: <list type="ordered">
                <item><hi rend="bold">petere</hi><hi rend="italic">aliquid ab aliquo</hi> [to ask
                    <hi rend="italic">for something from somebody</hi>] </item>
                <item><hi rend="bold">petere</hi><hi rend="italic">Romam</hi> [to rush <hi
                    rend="italic">to Rome</hi>]</item>
              </list></p>
            <p part="N">In theory, one could choose to encode such phenomena using <gi scheme="TEI"
                >gloss</gi>, but TEI Lex-0 recommends a clear separation of roles: <gi scheme="TEI"
                >gloss</gi> should be used for semantic or pragmatic information, whereas
              grammatical information should be encoded using the familiar <code
                rend="language-xpath">gramGrp/gram</code> constructs: </p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><sense n="1"
                xml:id="LD.peto.1"><gramGrp><gram type="government">aliquid ab
                  aliquo</gram></gramGrp></sense><sense n="1" xml:id="LD.peto.2"><gramGrp><gram
                    type="government">Romam</gram></gramGrp></sense></egXML>
            <p part="N">Here, too, it is important to note the possibility of ambiguity: unlike
              "petere aliquid ab aliquo", "petere Romam" could be interpreted as an example. The
              decision on such ambiguous cases should never be taken in isolation: editors of a
              digital edition need to consider the conventions of the dictionary as a whole before
              advising encoders on how to mark up such ambiguous cases. </p>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="senses.grammatical-properties.nested-entries-vs-multiple-senses" part="N"
            org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Nested entries vs. multiple senses</head>
            <p part="N">While TEI Lex-0 has been created to simplify the choices available for
              encoding various lexicographic components, certain levels of ambiguity remain, often
              due to the highly condensed nature of dictionary content.</p>
            <p part="N">Consider, for instance, this entry: <figure>
                <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/LlL0H4l.jpg"/>
              </figure></p>
            <p part="N">Is this an entry with two senses? Or are these two entries that were on the
              account of typographic density merged into one?</p>
            <p part="N">The answer is as much in the eyes of the beholder, as it is in the eyes of
              the lexicographers behind the dictionary that the entry stems from, in this case
                <title rend="italic">The Chambers Dictionary</title>. Both the encoder <hi
                rend="italic">and</hi> lexicographers, however, are influenced by lexicographic and
              linguistic traditions in which they operate. For an overview of the homonymy-polysemy
              dilemma, see, for instance, <ref target="#Zöfgen1989">Zöfgen 1989</ref>. </p>
            <p part="N">It can't be stressed enough that the goal of dictionary encoding is not to
              resolve linguistic disputes or evaluate lexicographic traditions but rather to create
              consistent, if abstracted, representations of lexicographic architectures. </p>
            <p part="N">So, what can we do in this particular case? Should we encode <hi rend="bold"
                >gash</hi> as an entry consisting of senses, each with a different part of speech,
              like this:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xml:id="gash-as-grammatical-homonyms"
              valid="true"><entry xml:id="CHDOEL.gash2" xml:lang="en"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"
                  ><!--this, as we'll explain later, is valid but not the preferred encoding--><form
                  type="lemma"><orth>gash</orth><pron>gash</pron></form><lbl type="homNum"
                  rend="sup">2</lbl><sense xml:id="CHDOEL.gash2.1"><pc>(</pc><usg
                    type="socioCultural" expand="slang">sl</usg><pc>)</pc><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                      >adj</gram></gramGrp><def>spare, extra</def><pc>.</pc></sense><metamark
                  function="senseSeparator">◆</metamark><sense xml:id="CDHDOEL.gash2.2"
                      ><gramGrp><gram type="pos">n</gram></gramGrp><pc>(</pc><usg type="temporal"
                    expand="originally">orig</usg><lbl>and esp</lbl><usg type="domain"
                    expand="nautical">naut</usg><pc>)</pc><def>rubbish,
                  waste</def><pc>.</pc></sense></entry></egXML>
            <p part="N">This is surely valid TEI Lex-0. There is conceptually nothing wrong with
              this encoding: it adequately represents the structure implied by the source text.</p>
            <p part="N"> We should, however, try to look at the issue at hand from a broader,
              comparative, perspective.</p>
            <list>
              <item>In the Portuguese polysemous entry <hi rend="bold">antepassado</hi><ref
                  target="#antepassado-gramGrp-in-sense">above</ref>, we had a case in which one
                particular sense (used in plural only) deviated from the other senses (which are
                used in both singular and plural). Since the senses were numbered in the original,
                there was never any doubt about how we would encode this. It was clear from the
                outset: <list>
                  <item>that the semantic information in that entry was grouped by a construct
                    called <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi>;</item>
                  <item>that senses inherited grammatical properties from the entry as a whole (i.e.
                      <code rend="language-xpath">entry/gramGrp</code>);</item>
                  <item>that, implicitly, we could assume that each sense can be used with the noun
                    in both singular and plural; and</item>
                  <item>that the plural-only sense was grammatically exceptional, hence <code
                      rend="language-xpath">entry/sense/gramGrp/</code>).</item>
                </list></item>
              <item>The English example is different: <hi rend="b">gash</hi> as a verb and as a noun
                are grammatical homonyms. If we encode them, as we did <ref
                  target="#gash-as-grammatical-homonyms">above</ref>, as two senses within one
                entry, we end up with an entry in which there is no inheritance (of grammatical
                properties) and only exceptions (at each sense-level). </item>
            </list>
            <p part="N">Because TEI Lex-0 is aimed at creating a baseline encoding to facilitate
              data exchange and comparison between different dictionaries, we, therefore, recommend
              to encode grammatical homonyms in TEI Lex-0 as nested entries and to use <gi
                scheme="TEI">gramGrp</gi> in <gi scheme="TEI">sense</gi> constructs to mark up
              sense-specific deviations from the rule of grammatical inheritance. </p>
            <p part="N">For that reason, our preferred encoding of <hi rend="bold">gash</hi> as a
              verb and a noun would be:</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="CH.gash2"
                xml:lang="en" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>gash</orth><pron>gash</pron></form><lbl type="homNum" rend="sup"
                  >2</lbl><entry xml:id="CH.gash2.1" xml:lang="en" type="homonymicEntry"><sense
                    xml:id="CH.gash2.1.1"><pc>(</pc><usg type="socioCultural" expand="slang"
                      >sl</usg><pc>)</pc><gramGrp><gram type="pos">adj</gram></gramGrp><def>spare,
                      extra</def><pc>.</pc></sense></entry><metamark function="entrySeparator"
                  >◆</metamark><entry xml:id="CH.gash2.2" xml:lang="en" type="homonymicEntry"
                      ><gramGrp><gram type="pos">n</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="CH.gahs2.2.1"
                      ><pc>(</pc><usg type="temporal" expand="originally">orig</usg><lbl>and
                      esp</lbl><usg type="domain" expand="nautical"
                      >naut</usg><pc>)</pc><def>rubbish,
                waste</def><pc>.</pc></sense></entry></entry></egXML>
            <p part="N">For an example in which grammatical homonyms have themselves multiple
              senses, one of which is grammatically constrained, see, for instance: </p>
            <figure>
              <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/b9dNR9m.png"/>
            </figure>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="ED.aid"
                xml:lang="en" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>aid</orth><pron>/ed/</pron></form><entry xml:id="ED.aid_n" xml:lang="en"
                  type="homonymicEntry"><gramGrp><gram type="pos">noun</gram></gramGrp><sense
                    xml:id="ED.aid_n.1" n="1"><num>1.</num><gramGrp><gram type="number"
                        value="singularia tantum"/></gramGrp><def>help, especially money, food or
                      other gifts given to people living in difficult conditions</def><metamark
                      function="exampleMarker">○</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>aid to the
                        earth-quake zone</quote></cit><cit type="example"><quote>an aid
                        worker</quote></cit><note>(NOTE: This meaning of aid has no
                      plural.)</note><metamark function="relatedEntryMarker">○</metamark><entry
                      type="relatedEntry" xml:id="ED.aid_n.1.in_aid_of" xml:lang="en"><form
                        type="lemma"><orth>in aid of</orth></form><sense
                        xml:id="ED.aid_n.1.in_aid_of.1"><def>in order to help</def><metamark
                          function="exampleMarker">○</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>We give
                            money in aid of the Red Cross.</quote></cit><metamark
                          function="exampleMarker">○</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>They are
                            collecting money in aid of
                    refugees.</quote></cit></sense></entry></sense><sense xml:id="ED.aid_n.2" n="2"
                      ><num>2.</num><def>thing which helps you to do something</def><metamark
                      function="exampleMarker">○</metamark><cit type="example"><quote>kitchen
                        aids</quote></cit></sense></entry><metamark function="subentryMarker"
                  >■</metamark><entry xml:id="ED.aid_v" xml:lang="en" type="homonymicEntry"
                      ><gramGrp><gram type="pos">verb</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="ED.aid.v.1"
                    n="1"><num>1.</num><def>to help something to happen</def></sense><sense
                    xml:id="ED.aid.v.2" n="2"><num>2.</num><def>to help
                  someone</def></sense></entry></entry></egXML>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="translations" xml:base="includes/25__translations.xml" xml:lang="" part="N"
        org="uniform" sample="complete">
        <head>Translations</head>
        <p part="N">TEI Guidelines:</p>
        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false"
                ><entry><form><orth>horrifier</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
              >v</gram></gramGrp><cit type="translation" xml:lang="en"><quote>to
              horrify</quote></cit><cit type="example"><quote>elle était horrifiée par la
                dépense</quote><cit type="translation" xml:lang="en"><quote>she was horrified at the
                  expense.</quote></cit></cit></entry></egXML>
        <p part="N">TEI Lex-0:</p>
        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="horrifier"
            type="mainEntry" xml:lang="fr" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
              type="lemma"><orth>horrifier</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
              >v</gram></gramGrp><sense xml:id="horrifier.1"><cit type="translationEquivalent"
                xml:lang="en"><form><orth>horrify</orth></form></cit><cit type="example"><quote>elle
                  était horrifiée par la dépense</quote><cit type="translation" xml:lang="en"
                    ><quote>she was horrified at the
          expense</quote></cit></cit></sense></entry></egXML>
        <figure>
          <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/IVutWGQ.png"/>
        </figure>
        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry type="mainEntry"
            xml:lang="en" xml:id="aid" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
              type="lemma"><orth>Aid</orth></form><pc>,</pc><sense xml:id="aid.1"><gramGrp><gram
                  type="pos">v.a.</gram></gramGrp><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                    ><form><orth>aider</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit
                type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                ><form><orth>assister</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit type="translationEquivalent"
                xml:lang="fr"><form><orth>secourir</orth></form></cit></sense><pc>;</pc><sense
              xml:id="aid.2"><gramGrp><gram type="pos">s.</gram></gramGrp><cit
                type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                ><form><orth>aide</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit type="translationEquivalent"
                xml:lang="fr"><form><orth>assistance</orth><pc>,</pc><gramGrp><gram type="gen"
                      >f.</gram></gramGrp></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit type="translationEquivalent"
                xml:lang="fr"><form><orth>secours</orth><pc>,</pc><gramGrp><gram type="gen"
                      >m.</gram></gramGrp></form></cit></sense><pc>;</pc><sense xml:id="aid.3"><cit
                type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                  ><form><orth>sub-side</orth></form><pc>,</pc><gramGrp><gram type="gender"
                    >m.</gram></gramGrp></cit></sense><pc>;</pc><sense xml:id="aid.4"
                ><gloss>(pers)</gloss><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                    ><form><orth>aide</orth></form><pc>,</pc><gramGrp><gram type="gen"
                    >m.</gram><gram type="gen">f.</gram></gramGrp></cit></sense><entry
              type="relatedEntry" xml:lang="en" xml:id="by_the_aid_of"><form type="lemma"><orth>By
                  the <ref type="oRef">_</ref> of</orth></form><pc>,</pc><sense
                xml:id="by_the_aid_of.1"><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                      ><form><orth>à l'aide de</orth></form></cit></sense></entry><pc>.</pc><entry
              type="relatedEntry" xml:lang="en" xml:id="in_aid_of"><form><orth>In <ref type="oRef"
                    >_</ref> of</orth></form><pc>,</pc><sense xml:id="in_aid_of.1"><gloss>(of
                  performances)</gloss><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                      ><form><orth>au profit de</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit
                  type="translationEquivalent"><form><orth>au bénéfice
                de</orth></form></cit></sense></entry><pc>.</pc><entry type="derived" xml:lang="en"
              xml:id="aidless"><form type="lemma"><orth>_less</orth><pc>,</pc><gramGrp><gram
                    type="pos">adj.</gram></gramGrp></form><sense xml:id="aidless.1"><cit
                  type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"><form><orth>sans
                  aide</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                      ><form><orth>sans secours</orth></form></cit></sense><pc>;</pc><sense
                xml:id="aidless.2"><cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                      ><form><orth>abandonné</orth></form></cit><pc>,</pc><cit
                  type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="fr"
                  ><form><orth>délaissé</orth></form></cit></sense></entry></entry></egXML>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="cross-references" xml:base="includes/30__cross-references.xml" xml:lang=""
        part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
        <head>Cross-references</head>
        <p part="N">The current TEI <hi rend="italic">Guidelines</hi> provide several mechanisms by
          means of which one item of lexical information can refer to another, e.g.:</p>
        <list type="unordered">
          <item><gi scheme="TEI">gloss</gi> for the provision of simple (non refined) translation
            equivalents of the head word</item>
          <item><code>&lt;usg type="synonym"/&gt;</code> for synonym references</item>
          <item><code>&lt;cit
              type="translation"&gt;&lt;quote&gt;&lt;!--...--&gt;&lt;/quote&gt;&lt;/cit&gt;</code>
            for translation equivalents in bilingual or translation dictionaries</item>
          <item><gi scheme="TEI">oRef</gi> and <gi scheme="TEI">pRef</gi> for the resolution of “~"
            headword placeholders in quotations and other dictionary text</item>
          <item><gi scheme="TEI">xr</gi> and <gi scheme="TEI">ref</gi> as a general
            cross-referencing mechanism</item>
          <item><code>&lt;ptr/&gt;</code> as a pointer to another location </item>
          <item><code>&lt;link/&gt;</code> element</item>
          <item><code>&lt;mentioned/&gt;</code> in the etymology section</item>
          <item><code>&lt;term/&gt;</code> for mentions of technical terms</item>
        </list>
        <p part="N">In keeping with the approach of the TEI Lex-0, and considering that
          links/relations between lexical data elements are an essential part of the core lexical
          data model rather than mere convenience pointers for dictionary users, we need a more
          unified and more constrained mechanism for lexical references, whether they point to an
          existing lexical entity in some dictionary or lexicon, or in a more general way to lexical
          objects without a target reference. </p>
        <p part="N">The proposed mechanism has the following properties </p>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>It applies only to references with a clear <hi rend="italic">linguistic</hi>
            meaning.</item>
          <item>The number of arbitrary (or context-dependent) choices for the encoder is minimal;
            the semantics of the reference should not depend on context</item>
          <item>The relation between representing dictionary content and the underlying/implied
            lexical data model should be as transparent as possible</item>
          <item>No drastic changes to the TEI Guidelines are needed.</item>
        </list>
        <p part="N">In the following section, we first present the recommended encoding, and then
          elicit how existing alternatives can be replaced accordingly.</p>
        <div xml:id="cross-references.xr-vs-ref" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>xr vs. ref</head>
          <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, we use <gi scheme="TEI">ref</gi> as the general element for a
            lexical reference and <gi scheme="TEI">xr</gi> as the enclosing element that groups all
            information related to this reference, including explicit labels such as "Syn.", "Cf.",
            "See also" etc. The reference may be internal to a dictionary or pointing to an external
            source, even when the actual target lexical object is not explicitly known. In the
            latter case, <gi scheme="TEI">ref</gi> can be used without an explicit pointing
            attribute. Furthermore, the intended target of the reference can be a full entry, but,
            sometimes, also a specific sense.</p>
          <p part="N">For all such uses, the following attributes may be used on <gi scheme="TEI"
              >xr</gi> and <gi scheme="TEI">ref</gi>:</p>
          <list type="unordered">
            <item><att scheme="TEI">type</att> is a mandatory attribute on <gi scheme="TEI">xr</gi>
              for a lexical reference. Its default value is "related". This attribute can be used to
              indicate the lexical relation between the headword of the entry and the object
              referred to (see next section) </item>
            <item><code rend="language-xpath">ref/@type</code> is required; it indicates the target
              object category (entry, sense); the <att scheme="TEI">type</att> attribute on <gi
                scheme="TEI">ref</gi> is also needed to distinguish lexicographic from bibliographic
              references..</item>
            <item><att scheme="TEI">xml:lang</att> on <gi scheme="TEI">xr</gi> is required when <gi
                scheme="TEI">ref</gi> contains an explicit lexical form in a language which is
              different from the source language</item>
            <item><code rend="language-xpath">ref/@target</code> to point to the URI of a lexical
              object. The value of this attribute is a machine-readable link to your
              cross-reference.</item>
            <item><code rend="language-xpath">ref/@notation</code> indicates, like we currently do
              on <gi scheme="TEI">orth</gi> or <gi scheme="TEI">pron</gi>, the notation used for the
              explicit lexical form, where applicable</item>
          </list>
          <p part="N">Explicit dictionary labels which indicate the type of relationship between the
            current lexical item and the cross-reference should be encoded as <gi scheme="TEI"
              >lbl</gi> inside of <gi scheme="TEI">xr</gi>. </p>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.xr-vs-ref.target" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Values of <code>ref/@target</code></head>
            <p part="N"><list type="bulleted">
                <item>If the reference has no explicit target, no <att scheme="TEI">target</att> is
                  used. </item>
                <item>As per <ref
                    target="http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SA.html#SAXP">TEI
                    pointing mechanisms</ref>, the value of <att scheme="TEI">target</att> must be
                  an URI reference. </item>
                <item>For internal references (references to the same dictionary), TEI Lex-0
                  enforces the use of explicit pointers to the <att scheme="TEI">xml:id</att> of an
                  element being pointed to, preceded by <code>#</code>. See Section "<ref
                    target="http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SA.html#SABN"
                    >Pointing Locally</ref>" in the TEI <hi rend="italic">Guidelines</hi>.</item>
                <item><ref
                    target="http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SA.html#SATSin">TEI
                    pointers</ref> should <hi rend="italic">not</hi> be used in TEI Lex-0. </item>
              </list></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div xml:id="cross-references.typology" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
          <head>Cross-reference typology</head>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.related" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Related</head>
            <p part="N"> The default reference to another lexical unit when no more granular
              information about the type of relationship is available.</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, cross-references are by default encoded as <code>&lt;xr
                type="related"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code>.</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:lang="nl"
                xml:id="borcht" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>borcht</orth></form><xr type="related"><lbl>Cf.</lbl><ref
                    target="#M012340" type="entry">burcht</ref></xr></entry></egXML>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.synonymy" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Synonymy</head>
            <p part="N">Relation between two lexical units X and Y which are syntactically identical
              and have the property that any declarative sentence S containing X has equivalent
              truth conditions to another sentence S’ which is identical to S, except that X is
              replaced by Y. (Adapted from <ref target="#Cruse1986">Cruse 1986</ref>.)</p>
            <p part="N">Synonymy is the linguistic parallel of the identity relation between
              classes. Synonyms differ in peripheral traits, related for example to stylistic,
              dialectal or diachronic variations.</p>
            <p part="N">Examples: [de] {Hund, Köter}, [en] {flashlight, torch}, [en] {glad, joyful,
              happy}, [en] {violin, fiddle} [en] He plays the violin very well/He plays the fiddle
              very well.</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, synonyms are encoded inside <code>&lt;xr
                type="synonymy"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code></p>
            <figure>
              <graphic width="100%" url="../../assets/images/imgur/yXte5h1.png"/>
            </figure>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#Duden2007" valid="true"><entry
                xml:id="arbeitsunfähig" xml:lang="de" type="mainEntry"
                xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form type="lemma"
                    ><orth>arbeitsunfähig</orth></form><sense xml:id="arbeitsunfähig.1"><xr
                    type="synonymy"><ref type="entry">bettlägerig</ref></xr><pc>,</pc><xr
                    type="synonymy"><ref type="entry">krank</ref></xr><pc>,</pc><xr type="synonymy"
                      ><ref type="entry">unpässlich</ref></xr><pc>;</pc></sense><sense
                  xml:id="arbeitsunfähig.2"><pc>(</pc><usg type="domain"
                    >bildungsspr.</usg><pc>):</pc><xr type="synonymy"><ref type="entry"
                      >indisponiert</ref></xr></sense><sense xml:id="arbeitsunfähig.3"><xr
                    type="synonymy"><pc>(</pc><lbl>oft</lbl><usg type="attitude"
                      >emotional</usg><pc>):</pc><ref type="entry"
                  >malade</ref></xr><pc>.</pc></sense></entry></egXML>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.hypernymy" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Hypernymy</head>
            <p part="N">Relation between lexical heads X and Y characterised by the property that
              the sentence This is a(n) Y entails, but is not entailed by the sentence This is a(n)
              X. (Adapted from <ref target="#Cruse1986">Cruse 1986</ref>.) </p>
            <p part="N">Hypernymy is the converse of hyponymy.</p>
            <p part="N"> Example: dog/animal (animal is a hypernym of dog)</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, hypernyms are encoded inside <code>&lt;xr
                type="hypernymy"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code>.</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><entry xml:id="XY.dog"
                xml:lang="en" type="mainEntry" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"><form
                  type="lemma"><orth>dog</orth></form><gramGrp><gram type="pos"
                  >n</gram></gramGrp><xr type="hypernymy"><ref type="entry"
                >mammal</ref></xr></entry></egXML>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.hyponymy" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Hyponymy</head>
            <p part="N">Relation between lexical units X and Y characterised by the property that
              the sentence This is a(n) X entails, but is not entailed by the sentence This is a(n)
              Y. (Adapted from <ref target="#Cruse1986">Cruse 1986</ref>.)</p>
            <p part="N">Hyponymy and its converse hypernymy are the linguistic parallels of the
              relation of inclusion between two classes.</p>
            <p part="N">Examples: [en] animal/dog, red/scarlet, to kill/to murder</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, hyponyms are encoded inside <code>&lt;xr
                type="hyponymy"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code>.</p>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.meronymy" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Meronymy</head>
            <p part="N">An inclusion relation between lexical heads X and Y which reflect a
              potential part-whole relation between their referents in discourse. (Adapted from <ref
                target="#Cruse2011">Cruse 2011, p. 140</ref>)</p>
            <p part="N">Example: finger:hand (finger is said to be a meronym of hand, and hand is
              said to be the holonym of finger).</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, meornyms are encoded inside <code>&lt;xr
                type="meronymy"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code>.</p>
          </div>
          <div xml:id="cross-references.typology.antonymy" part="N" org="uniform" sample="complete">
            <head>Antonymy</head>
            <p part="N">Relation between lexical units of opposite meaning.</p>
            <p part="N">In TEI Lex-0, antonyms are encoded inside <code>&lt;xr
                type="antonymy"&gt;&lt;/xr&gt;</code>.</p>
            <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="true"><sense
                xml:id="DLPC.antepassado_a_1" xml:base="../examples/examples.stripped.xml"
                xml:lang="pt"><def>Que pertence ou viveu numa época anterior.</def><xr
                  type="synonymy"><ref type="sense">antecessor</ref></xr><xr type="synonymy"><ref
                    type="sense">sucessor</ref></xr><xr type="antonymy"><ref type="sense"
                    >descendente</ref></xr><xr type="antonymy"><ref type="sense"
                >sucessor</ref></xr></sense></egXML>
          </div>
        </div>
        <!--TODO: reuse examples elsewhere, if needed. Especially oRef must be highlighted! -->
        <!--<div>
